var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#The-AAA-fit","page":"AAA","text":"AAA() finds zeros of f by sampling f on a grid z_1dotsz_m and  fitting a rational function to y_i = 1f(z_i). It reads the zeros of f off as the poles of the fit; each candidate is then confirmed by the winding on a small circle around, and polished.  This page describes the fit and what it costs.","title":"The AAA fit"},{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#The-AAA-iteration","page":"AAA","text":"AAA [1] writes the fit in barycentric form over n support points sigma_1dotssigma_n chosen among the samples,\n\nr(z) = fracsum_k=1^n dfracw_k y_sigma_kz - z_sigma_k\n            sum_k=1^n dfracw_kz - z_sigma_k \n\na rational function of degree n-1 that interpolates y at the support points for any nonzero weights w. The weights are free to make r match the other samples. Multiplying y_i - r(z_i) by the denominator makes the mismatch linear in w: it is (Lw)_i with the Loewner matrix\n\nL_ik = fracy_i - y_sigma_kz_i - z_sigma_k qquad i notin sigma \n\nso w is taken as the unit vector minimizing lVert LwrVert, the right singular vector of the smallest singular value. The iteration is greedy: the sample where y_i - r(z_i) is largest becomes the next support point, until the mismatch falls below tolcdotmaxy. The poles are the zeros of the denominator, the finite eigenvalues of an (n+1)times(n+1) arrowhead pencil.\n\nComputing w from a fresh SVD at step n costs O(m n^2), so a fit that ends at N support points costs O(m N^3). A box holding dozens of zeros needs N sim 100, where the SVDs can outweigh the evaluations of f.","title":"The AAA iteration"},{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#Updating-instead-of-refactoring","page":"AAA","text":"Between consecutive steps L changes in two small ways. Choosing sample p as the new support point\n\nremoves row p, which is a rank-one change, and\nappends one column, the Loewner column of p.\n\nA QR factorization can follow both changes in O(mn) [2], [3, §6.5]; Lietaert et al. [7] update AAA's Loewner QR the same way. Keep L = QR with Q in mathbbC^mtimes n having orthonormal columns and R upper triangular, and let removed rows stay in place as zero rows, so no index bookkeeping is needed.\n\nZeroing row p. Let q = Q^H e_p and u = (e_p - Qq)tau with tau = lVert e_p - QqrVert. Then Q  u has orthonormal columns, its range contains e_p, and its row p is q^H  tau. Apply n Givens rotations, each mixing column j with the last column for j = ndots1, chosen to move row p onto 0 cdots 0  1. Afterwards the last column is e_p itself, since it has unit norm and a unit entry, and the first n columns Q vanish in row p. The same rotations applied to the rows of beginbmatrix R  0 endbmatrix give beginbmatrix R  v^T endbmatrix with R still upper triangular, because the order j = ndots1 only fills the extra row. So\n\nL = QR + e_p v^T \n\nwhere v^T is the old row p of L, and QR is L with that row zeroed. Each rotation touches two columns of length m and two rows of length at most n: O(mn) in total.\n\nAppending a column. Orthogonalize the new column a against Q by classical Gram–Schmidt, r = Q^Ha, a leftarrow a - Qr; the coefficients r and the remaining norm form the new column of R. Near convergence the new column is almost in the range of Q, which is exactly when one pass loses orthogonality, so the pass is repeated while the remainder keeps collapsing; two passes are enough [4]. Cost O(mn).","title":"Updating instead of refactoring"},{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#Weights-by-inverse-iteration","page":"AAA","text":"The smallest right singular vector of L is that of R, and for a triangular matrix it comes cheaply from inverse iteration on R^HR [5]: each sweep w leftarrow R^-1R^-Hw, then normalize, is two triangular solves, O(n^2). A sweep damps every other singular direction by (sigma_nsigma_j)^2. Convergence to the singular vector is slow when sigma_n-1approxsigma_n, but the fit does not need the vector: it needs lVert RwrVert small, and directions that survive the damping have sigma_j approx sigma_n by construction. A fixed handful of sweeps therefore gives weights as good as the SVD's.\n\nThe two triangular solves work on R and R^H separately rather than on the Gram matrix L^HL. That matters: a converged fit has sigma_nsigma_1 sim tol = 10^-13, and factoring L^HL would square it past machine precision. Updating a Cholesky factor of the Gram matrix is the route taken by FastAAA [6], which suits looser tolerances than a zero search can afford.","title":"Weights by inverse iteration"},{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#Scaling","page":"AAA","text":"With m samples and a fit ending at N support points:\n\nstage refactoring every step updating\nLoewner factorization O(mN^3) O(mN^2)\nweights O(N^4) O(N^3)\nmismatch at the samples O(mN^2) O(mN^2)\npoles, once per fit O(N^3) O(N^3)\n\nThe fit drops from O(mN^3) to O(mN^2), a factor that grows with the number of roots in the box. What remains at large N is the pole eigenproblem.","title":"Scaling"},{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#What-the-fit-can-see","page":"AAA","text":"A zero z_0 of f is a pole of 1f with residue 1f(z_0). The fit resolves it only if that residue stands above tolcdotmax1f at the samples it keeps: samples where 1f is zero, non-finite, or above the median of its nearest samples by more than 1sqrtmathrmtol (a sample on a pole of 1f) are dropped. Zeros where f is exponentially large are found or missed depending on details of the sampling, so a zero set is reproducible only above this threshold.","title":"What the fit can see"},{"category":"section","location":"aaa/#References","page":"AAA","text":"Y. Nakatsukasa, O. Sète, L. N. Trefethen, *The AAA algorithm for\n\nrational approximation*, SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 40, A1494 (2018).\n\nJ. W. Daniel, W. B. Gragg, L. Kaufman, G. W. Stewart, *Reorthogonalization\n\nand stable algorithms for updating the Gram–Schmidt QR factorization*, Math. Comp.   30, 772 (1976).\n\nG. H. Golub, C. F. Van Loan, Matrix Computations, 4th ed., Johns Hopkins\n\nUniversity Press (2013).\n\nL. Giraud, J. Langou, M. Rozložník, J. van den Eshof, *Rounding error\n\nanalysis of the classical Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization process*, Numer. Math. 101,   87 (2005).\n\nI. C. F. Ipsen, Computing an eigenvector with inverse iteration, SIAM Rev.\n\n39, 254 (1997).\n\nA. Hochman, FastAAA: A fast rational-function fitter, IEEE EPEPS (2017).\nP. Lietaert, J. Pérez, B. Vandereycken, K. Meerbergen, *Automatic rational\n\napproximation and linearization of nonlinear eigenvalue problems*, IMA J. Numer. Anal.   42, 1087 (2022).","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.AAA","page":"API","text":"AAA(; nsamples = 320, tol = 1e-13, max_degree = 150, stagnation = 5)\n\nZeros and poles of f(z) from a rational fit to its samples. A box zdomain is sampled evenly at about nsamples points, and confirming a candidate near its edge may evaluate f slightly outside; an array zdomain is the samples, with zeros and poles reported wherever the fit finds them.\n\nnsamples — the fit finds at most about half as many zeros and poles as it has samples, and fewer in practice: 60–100 have needed 1000–4000. Too few miss some silently, with fitconverged = true. Use more for features narrower than the spacing.\ntol — the fit stops once it matches the samples to tol relative to their largest magnitude; a zero or pole whose effect on them is smaller goes unfound. Noise in f above tol stalls the fit (fitconverged = false); zeros and poles buried in it are lost.\nmax_degree, stagnation — the fit stops at this degree, or after this many steps without improving.\n\nSamples where f is NaN are skipped: return NaN where f cannot be trusted. unresolved lists candidates where f is too noisy or undefined to confirm them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.AAA"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.ArgumentPrinciple","page":"API","text":"ArgumentPrinciple(; zres, minsep = nothing, zoom = 4)\n\nZeros and poles of f(z) in the box zdomain = (zlo, zhi) by adaptive refinement of cells where the phase of f winds.\n\nzres — resolution of the points before the polish, and the scale at which loci are told apart: loci closer than about zres fuse into one of their net winding, e.g. two simple zeros read as one double zero.\nminsep — cells are refined to this size before any is discarded. A zero and a pole closer than minsep can cancel inside one cell and vanish silently, so keep it below their closest spacing; below zres, it is the resolution in place of zres. Loci of one kind closer than minsep can escape the search; the box's count recovers them at extra cost (deficit below). minsep also sets the cost floor, about 2·area/minsep² evaluations. nothing means the box diagonal / 8, or zres if that is coarser.\nzoom — halvings below zres, at most 8, to separate a fused zero–pole pair. A pair of equal orders closer than zres/2^zoom is reported unresolved; one of different orders closer than about zres stays fused, as one locus of their net winding.\n\nBesides the common fields, returns deficit: zeros minus poles in the box that the loci found (both kinds, as findzeropoles reports them) leave unaccounted. Nonzero means missed loci, often a zero or pole of order ≥ 4: divide known factors out of f. nothing means the winding around the box can't be read: f is non-finite on its edge, or a locus sits there (pad the box). A zero–pole pair cancels in the count, so deficit can't see one missed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.ArgumentPrinciple"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.Branch","page":"API","text":"Branch{M} <: AbstractVector{Sample{M}}\n\nOne locus z*(p) followed over M parameters, as Samples sorted by parameter.\n\nb.winding: the multiplicity; 0 for a lone sample whose kind could not be read.\nb.degree: how many loci the branch joins at one parameter point (Several parameters); 1 for a single-valued z*(p).\nb.uncertified: stretches (p1, p2) where another locus came within zfloor, so the branch may have switched to it, and (p, p) where following stopped at p (What is certified).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.Branch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.Continuation","page":"API","text":"Continuation(seed; zres = nothing, zfloor = nothing, certify = true)\n\nLoci over parameters for findloci: the point finder seed finds them at the knots, and muller follows them between. How it works and what it can miss.\n\nzres — the scale of a step's correction. nothing takes seed.zres of an ArgumentPrinciple seed, and a quarter of the sample spacing of an AAA seed.\nzfloor — loci closer than this are not told apart (uncertified of a Branch); two closer throughout are one locus. At most zres; nothing takes zres/256.\ncertify — count the zeros minus poles in the box at each knot against the branches (deficit of the Survey). Always on for an ArgumentPrinciple seed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.Continuation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.Muller","page":"API","text":"Muller(; tol = 1e-10, maxiter = 100)\n\nmuller polish for the simple zeros and poles from each estimate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.Muller"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.Sample","page":"API","text":"Sample{M}\n\nA point (z, p...) of a locus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.Sample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.Survey","page":"API","text":"Survey{M} <: AbstractVector{Branch{M}}\n\nThe Branches over M parameters. s.deficit lists p => n at the knots where n = N(p) - sum_b w_b, the zeros minus poles in the box less the windings of the branches there, is nonzero (The count), and p => nothing where N(p) cannot be read. n  0 means the seed missed loci at p. s.nevals counts the evaluations of f(p...)(z).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.Survey"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.findloci-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Union{AAA, ArgumentPrinciple}}","page":"API","text":"findloci(f, zdomain, pdomain, alg; mapper = map) :: Survey\n\nLoci z*(p) of the zeros and poles of f(p...)(z) in the box zdomain = (zlo, zhi) as p ranges over pdomain.\n\npdomain holds one entry per parameter: an interval (lo, hi), searched at equispaced knots (9 for one parameter, 5 each for more), or a sorted vector of knots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.findloci"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.findpoles-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"findpoles(f, zdomain, alg; polish = Muller(), mapper = map)\n\nPoles of f(z); otherwise as findzeros.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.findpoles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.findzeropoles-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"findzeropoles(f, zdomain, alg; polish = Muller(), mapper = map)\n\nZeros and poles of f(z) from one search, costing little more than either alone; otherwise as findzeros.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.findzeropoles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.findzeros-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"findzeros(f, zdomain, alg; polish = Muller(), mapper = map)\n\nZeros of f(z) in the box zdomain = (zlo, zhi) of opposite corners. Returns a named tuple:\n\npoints: sorted by real, then imaginary part.\nwindings: multiplicity, negative for a pole. Points closer than alg resolves merge into one of their summed winding.\nconverged: whether polish (Muller) met its tolerance. Only points of winding ±1 are polished; polish = nothing keeps the estimates of alg.\nunresolved: zeros or poles detected but not classified.\nnevals: evaluations of f.\nfitconverged (AAA) or deficit (ArgumentPrinciple).\n\nZeros and poles where f overflows to Inf or underflows to 0 go unfound: divide out exponential growth, or shrink the box.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.findzeros"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#MeromorphicLoci.muller-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"muller(f, x0; h, radius = Inf, tol = 1e-10, maxiter = 100)\nmuller(f, x0, x1, x2; tol = 1e-10, maxiter = 100)\n\nMuller's method for a zero of f, from a guess x0 uncertain by about h or from three starting points. Returns (; root, converged, nevals): root is the smallest-|f| point evaluated (NaN if none was finite), a zero when converged, i.e. a step fell below tol·max(|root|, h) (h the starting points' spread). Steps stay within radius of x0; keep h and radius below the distance to any other zero, or it may converge there.\n\nWhere noise or rounding in f limits accuracy above tol, as at a multiple zero, root lands about that limit away, typically unconverged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MeromorphicLoci.muller"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#How-ArgumentPrinciple-works","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"ArgumentPrinciple finds the zeros and poles (loci) of f in a box in four steps:\n\nSearch: refine a mesh of cells toward loci, reading only the phase of f at cell corners.\nCertificate: count the box's loci by its winding; search again where the cells fall short.\nLabel: group the cells and count each group by the argument principle.\nEscalate: refine groups whose label is unclear.","title":"How ArgumentPrinciple works"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#Cells","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"The box is tiled by near-square root cells of diagonal at most minsep.\nCells halve down to zres, and up to zoom levels further when escalating.\nCells stay near-square whatever the box's shape: four corners of a long, thin cell miss phase turns.","title":"Cells"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#Search","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"Cells are refined breadth-first. A cell fires, and is split further, when either check says it may hold a locus:\n\nWalk: step the quadrant of f around its four corners. A nonzero winding fires (+4 for a simple zero, −4 for a simple pole), and so does a jump across two quadrants, whose direction is ambiguous.\nGuard: the corners, plus the midpoints of edges that change quadrant, show at least 3 quadrants. Then both Re f = 0 and Im f = 0 cross the cell, and they meet only at loci.\n\nFiring cells split down to zres. The rest go dark.\n\n<details><summary>Code</summary>\n\nusing MeromorphicLoci, CairoMakie, Base64\nusing MeromorphicLoci: _search, _certify!, _group, _outline, _outlined, _mixed, _deepen!,\n    _polygon_winding, _cellwinding, _qstep, quadrant, Cell\n\nconst GREEN, RED, GREY = \"#389826\", \"#CB3C33\", \"#777777\"\nconst QCOLORS = [\"#E69F00\", \"#56B4E9\", \"#009E73\", \"#CC79A7\"]   # quadrants 1–4\nconst BOX = (-1 - 1im, 1 + 1im)\nsigncolor(w) = isnothing(w) || w == 0 ? GREY : w > 0 ? GREEN : RED\nsigned(w) = isnothing(w) ? \"unusable\" : w > 0 ? \"+$w\" : w < 0 ? \"−$(-w)\" : \"0\"\n\n# Shown at its logical width, so text keeps its size, and rendered at twice that. Inlined\n# as a data URI: a relative src breaks under prettyurls, which nest each page a level down.\nCairoMakie.activate!(px_per_unit=2)\nfunction embed(fig, name)\n    png = base64encode(io -> show(io, MIME\"image/png\"(), fig))\n    HTML(\"<img src=\\\"data:image/png;base64,$png\\\" width=\\\"$(size(fig.scene)[1])\\\" alt=\\\"$name\\\">\")\nend\n\nrect(lat, c::Cell) = Rect2f(reim(lat[c.base])..., c.step[1] * lat.zh[1], c.step[2] * lat.zh[2])\ngrid(lo, hi, n) = (range(real(lo), real(hi), n), range(imag(lo), imag(hi), n))\nfunction phase!(ax, f, lo, hi; n=400)\n    xs, ys = grid(lo, hi, n)\n    heatmap!(ax, xs, ys, [angle(f(complex(x, y))) for x in xs, y in ys];\n        colormap=:phase, colorrange=(-π, π), alpha=0.55)\n    limits!(ax, real(lo), real(hi), imag(lo), imag(hi))\nend\nfunction loci!(ax, zeros, poles; markersize=13)\n    scatter!(ax, reim.(zeros); marker=:circle, color=:white, strokecolor=:black, strokewidth=1.5, markersize)\n    scatter!(ax, reim.(poles); marker=:xcross, color=:black, markersize)\nend\n# The mesh the search has reached: its dark cells, its evaluations, and a marker on each firing cell.\nfunction mesh!(ax, memo, cells, dark)\n    lat = memo.lat\n    poly!(ax, rect.(Ref(lat), dark); color=:transparent, strokecolor=(:black, 0.45), strokewidth=0.5)\n    scatter!(ax, [Point2f(reim(lat[ix])...) for ix in keys(memo.slot)]; color=:black, markersize=2)\n    scatter!(ax, [Point2f(reim(lat[c.base.+c.step./2])...) for c in cells]; marker=:rect, color=:black, strokecolor=:white, strokewidth=1, markersize=9)\nend\n# Outline of a group in the z-plane, its vote, the points the vote evaluated, and whether it is mixed.\nfunction outline(memo, cells)\n    lat = memo.lat\n    vs, edges = _outline(lat, cells)\n    pts = NTuple{2,Float64}[]\n    _polygon_winding(p -> (append!(pts, p); memo(p)), vs, edges)\n    w, lo, hi = _outlined(memo, cells)\n    segs = [Point2f(reim(lat[vs[i]])...) for e in edges for i in e]\n    return (; segs, w, mixed=!isnothing(w) && _mixed(memo, cells, lo, hi), pts=[Point2f(reim(lat[p])...) for p in pts])\nend\n\nf = z -> (z - 0.3) * (z + 0.4im)^2 / (z - 0.6)\nmemo, cells, dark = _search(f, BOX, ArgumentPrinciple(zres=0.05), map)\nfig = Figure(; size=(420, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; aspect=DataAspect(), xlabel=\"Re z\", ylabel=\"Im z\")\nphase!(ax, f, BOX...)\nmesh!(ax, memo, cells, dark)\n\n</details>\n\nHTML{String}(\"<img src=\\\"data:image/png;base64,\\\" width=\\\"420\\\" alt=\\\"search\\\">\")\n\nThe search for (z − 0.3)(z + 0.4i)²/(z − 0.6) at zres = 0.05, over the phase of f (hue). Dots are evaluations, squares the cells firing at zres.\n\nThe guard is needed because the walk reads each edge as the shortest step: a +270° turn reads −90°. A neighbouring locus can bend an edge that far (middle panel below). Neither check sees a 4-fold zero centred in a cell (right): each edge turns a full 2π, so all corners share one quadrant.\n\n<details><summary>Code</summary>\n\n# One cell over the quadrant of f. Corners are in `_quadrants` order.\nfunction cell!(ax, f, lo, hi; zeros=ComplexF64[], view=(lo, hi))\n    h = hi - lo\n    pad = 0.3 * max(real(view[2] - view[1]), imag(view[2] - view[1]))\n    vlo, vhi = view[1] - pad * (1 + im), view[2] + pad * (1 + im)\n    xs, ys = grid(vlo, vhi, 500)\n    heatmap!(ax, xs, ys, [quadrant(f(complex(x, y))) for x in xs, y in ys];\n        colormap=cgrad(QCOLORS, 4; categorical=true), colorrange=(0.5, 4.5), alpha=0.4)\n    limits!(ax, real(vlo), real(vhi), imag(vlo), imag(vhi))\n    corners = [lo, lo + real(h), lo + imag(h) * im, hi]\n    q = quadrant.(f.(corners))\n    lines!(ax, reim.(corners[[1, 2, 4, 3, 1]]); color=:black, linewidth=2)\n    w = _cellwinding(q)\n    walkfires = isnothing(w) || w != 0\n    seen = Set(q)\n    guarded = !walkfires && length(seen) < 3   # the only cells whose midpoints are read\n    for (i, j) in ((1, 2), (2, 4), (4, 3), (3, 1))\n        m = (corners[i] + corners[j]) / 2\n        out = (m - (lo + hi) / 2) / abs(m - (lo + hi) / 2)\n        step = _qstep(q[i], q[j])\n        text!(ax, reim(m + 0.12 * abs(h) * out)...; text=isnothing(step) ? \"±2\" : signed(step),\n            align=(:center, :center), fontsize=15, font=:bold)\n        guarded && q[i] != q[j] || continue\n        push!(seen, quadrant(f(m)))\n        scatter!(ax, reim(m)...; marker=:diamond, color=QCOLORS[quadrant(f(m))], strokecolor=:black, strokewidth=1.5, markersize=15)\n    end\n    scatter!(ax, reim.(corners); color=QCOLORS[q], strokecolor=:black, strokewidth=1.5, markersize=17)\n    loci!(ax, zeros, ComplexF64[])\n    ax.title = walkfires ? \"walk $(isnothing(w) ? \"ambiguous\" : signed(w)): fires\" :\n               length(seen) ≥ 3 ? \"walk 0, $(length(seen)) quadrants: guard fires\" : \"walk 0, one quadrant: dark\"\nend\n\nfig = Figure(; size=(760, 300))\naxs = [Axis(fig[1, i]; aspect=DataAspect()) for i in 1:3]\nhidedecorations!.(axs)\na = 0.37 + 0.62im\ncell!(axs[1], z -> (z - a) * cis(0.4), 0im, 1 + 1im; zeros=[a])\n# a pair of test/runtests.jl, and the lattice cell of `BOX` that holds `a`\na, b = 0.367 + 0.069im, 0.182 + 0.075im\ncell!(axs[2], z -> (z - a) * (z - b), 0.25 + 0im, 0.375 + 0.125im; zeros=[a, b], view=(real(b) + 0im, 0.375 + 0.125im))\ncell!(axs[3], z -> (z - 0.5 - 0.5im)^4 * cis(0.5), 0im, 1 + 1im; zeros=[0.5 + 0.5im])\nLegend(fig[2, :], [PolyElement(; color=(c, 0.6)) for c in QCOLORS], [\"quadrant $i\" for i in 1:4];\n    orientation=:horizontal, framevisible=false)\n\n</details>\n\nHTML{String}(\"<img src=\\\"data:image/png;base64,\\\" width=\\\"760\\\" alt=\\\"walk_guard\\\">\")\n\nCorners (circles) and guard midpoints (diamonds) over the quadrant of f, with each edge's quadrant step. Left: one simple zero. Middle: one of two zeros (○). Right: a 4-fold zero.","title":"Search"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#Certificate","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"The winding of f around the box counts its loci, zeros minus poles.\n\nThe search passes if the firing cells, grown by one cell, wind the same.\nOtherwise each dark cell is checked by its own winding. It holds no firing cell, so a nonzero winding is a missed locus: the cell is searched again, and fires if it still winds at zres.\nWhat the labels (below) leave of the box's count is reported as deficit (see ArgumentPrinciple).\n\n<details><summary>Code</summary>\n\n# Eight zeros inside one root cell, before and after the count is checked.\nc = 0.3 + 0.2im\nf = z -> (z - c)^8 - 0.1^8\nalg = ArgumentPrinciple(zres=0.02, minsep=0.36)\nmemo, cells, dark = _search(f, BOX, alg, map)\nfig = Figure(; size=(720, 380))\nfor (i, title) in enumerate((\"search\", \"after the certificate\"))\n    i == 2 && _certify!(memo, cells, dark, alg.zres)\n    local ax = Axis(fig[1, i]; aspect=DataAspect(), xlabel=\"Re z\", ylabel=\"Im z\", title=\"$title: $(length(cells)) firing cells\")\n    phase!(ax, f, BOX...)\n    mesh!(ax, memo, cells, dark)\nend\n\n</details>\n\nHTML{String}(\"<img src=\\\"data:image/png;base64,\\\" width=\\\"720\\\" alt=\\\"certificate\\\">\")\n\nEight zeros on a circle 0.1 across, inside one root cell (minsep = 0.36). The search finds some; the box counts 8, so the dark cells that wind are searched again.","title":"Certificate"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#Labels","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"Firing cells at zres that share an edge or a corner form a group.\nA group's label is the winding of f around its outline: its cells grown by one cell and clipped to the box. Separate groups are at least two cells apart, so an outline holds no other group's cells.\nOutlines are integrated adaptively: each edge is bisected until every step turns the phase by less than π/2 and changes |f| by less than 2×.\nA group is mixed if its vote may hide loci of both kinds, e.g. a simple zero beside a double pole votes −1. By the maximum modulus principle, a cell corner with |f| above the outline's maximum implies a pole inside, below its minimum a zero; both means mixed.\n\n<details><summary>Code</summary>\n\nzs, ps = [0.10 + 0.07im, -0.2 - 0.15im], [0.27 + 0.12im]\nf = z -> (z - zs[1]) * (z - zs[2])^2 / (z - ps[1])\nmemo, cells, _ = _search(f, BOX, ArgumentPrinciple(zres=0.05), map)\nfig = Figure(; size=(500, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; aspect=DataAspect(), xlabel=\"Re z\", ylabel=\"Im z\")\nphase!(ax, f, -0.42 - 0.36im, 0.5 + 0.32im)\nfor g in _group(cells)\n    o = outline(memo, g)\n    poly!(ax, rect.(Ref(memo.lat), g); color=(signcolor(o.w), 0.55), strokecolor=:black, strokewidth=0.5)\n    linesegments!(ax, o.segs; color=:black, linewidth=2.5)\n    scatter!(ax, o.pts; color=:white, strokecolor=:black, strokewidth=1, markersize=6)\n    text!(ax, minimum(first, o.segs), maximum(last, o.segs); text=\"w = $(signed(o.w))\", fontsize=16, font=:bold, align=(:left, :bottom))\nend\nloci!(ax, zs, ps; markersize=10)\n\n</details>\n\nHTML{String}(\"<img src=\\\"data:image/png;base64,\\\" width=\\\"500\\\" alt=\\\"labels\\\">\")\n\nGroups of firing cells (filled), their outlines, and the points each vote evaluated, for a simple zero, a simple pole and a double zero at zres = 0.05.","title":"Labels"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#Escalation","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"A group that is mixed, votes 0, or cannot be read is refined one level below zres, regrouped and labelled again, up to zoom levels. At the last level a nonzero vote stands, mixed or not.\nA group that votes 0 or cannot be read, and has nothing firing one level finer, is dropped: its cells fired on a misread phase (a locus just outside the box, a phase turning faster than a cell), not on a locus.\nA group still unlabelled at the last level is reported unresolved: a zero and a pole closer than a last-level cell.\nEach labelled group is reported at the mean of its cell centres, then polished.\n\n<details><summary>Code</summary>\n\n# One row per function, one panel per level until every group is labelled.\nrows = [\n    (; f=z -> (z - 0.985 - 0.011im) / (z - 1.012 - 0.006im), box=(0 - 0.4im, 2 + 0.4im), zres=0.05,\n        zeros=[0.985 + 0.011im], poles=[1.012 + 0.006im], view=(0.94 - 0.04im, 1.06 + 0.06im)),\n    (; f=z -> (z - 0.52 - 0.33im) / (z - 0.52 - 0.33im - 0.02cispi(0.25))^2, box=BOX, zres=0.02,\n        zeros=[0.52 + 0.33im], poles=[0.52 + 0.33im + 0.02cispi(0.25)], view=(0.488 + 0.305im, 0.572 + 0.375im)),\n]\nfig = Figure(; size=(580, 480))\nfor (r, (; f, box, zres, zeros, poles, view)) in enumerate(rows)\n    local memo, cells = _search(f, box, ArgumentPrinciple(; zres), map)\n    undecided(g) = (o = outline(memo, g); o.mixed || something(o.w, 0) == 0)\n    levels = [_group(cells)]\n    while any(undecided, levels[end]) && length(levels) < 3\n        push!(levels, reduce(vcat, [undecided(g) ? _group(_deepen!(memo, g)) : [g] for g in levels[end]]))\n    end\n    for (i, groups) in enumerate(levels)\n        local ax = Axis(fig[r, i]; aspect=DataAspect(), title=i == 1 ? \"zres\" : \"zres / $(2^(i - 1))\")\n        hidedecorations!(ax)\n        phase!(ax, f, view...)\n        for g in groups\n            o = outline(memo, g)\n            poly!(ax, rect.(Ref(memo.lat), g); color=(signcolor(o.w), 0.6), strokecolor=:black, strokewidth=0.5)\n            linesegments!(ax, o.segs; color=:black, linewidth=2.5)\n            left = something(o.w, 0) ≥ 0    # opposite corners, so neighbouring labels stay apart\n            text!(ax, (left ? minimum : maximum)(first, o.segs), maximum(last, o.segs); text=\"w = $(signed(o.w))\" * (o.mixed ? \", mixed\" : \"\"),\n                fontsize=15, font=:bold, align=(left ? :left : :right, :bottom))\n        end\n        loci!(ax, zeros, poles; markersize=10)\n    end\nend\n\n</details>\n\nHTML{String}(\"<img src=\\\"data:image/png;base64,\\\" width=\\\"580\\\" alt=\\\"escalation\\\">\")\n\nTop: a zero (○) and a pole (×) 0.027 apart at zres = 0.05 form one group voting 0; two levels finer they split. Bottom: a zero beside a double pole 0.02 apart at zres = 0.02 votes −1, mixed; one level finer they split. Cells are green, red or grey as their group votes positive, negative or 0.","title":"Escalation"},{"category":"section","location":"argument_principle/#What-can-go-wrong","page":"ArgumentPrinciple","text":"Case Effect Recovered by\nZero–pole pair much closer than minsep cancels on the walk and in the box's count; its phase fades within a few pair-widths nothing\nSame-sign loci closer than minsep, or phase turning > π/2 between corners walk reads 0 certificate\nk-fold locus centred in a cell walk misreads it (triple −4, 4-fold 0) at every scale, since (z − z0)^k is scale-free certificate\nSimple locus with a second one hugging the same edge can slip past walk and guard certificate, or fused into its partner's label\nk-fold locus on a lattice point only cells beside it fire; outlines through it can't be read and it is dropped flagged by deficit\nZero and pole of different orders closer than about a cell (two for order 3) not mixed: labelled one locus of their net winding nothing (the count agrees)","title":"What can go wrong"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#continuation","page":"Continuation","text":"Write f(z p) for f(p)(z), f = partial_z f, B for the box in z, and p_1  dots  p_K for the knots. Continuation(seed) finds the zeros and poles of f(cdot p_k) in B with a point finder, the seeder, and follows each from knot to knot.","title":"How Continuation follows loci"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#Following-one-locus","page":"Continuation","text":"A simple zero z^*(p) continues analytically while f(z^* p) ne 0: until it meets another zero or a pole, or leaves B. A pole is followed as a zero of 1f, and a locus of constant multiplicity m ge 2 as a simple zero of ff.\n\nEach step extrapolates the last samples (degree le 2 in p) to the next p and corrects with Muller's method. A track ends at the next knot, on partial B, or stuck: no step is accepted however short.","title":"Following one locus"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#What-is-certified","page":"Continuation","text":"A Branch asserts that its samples lie on one locus. Write z_j for the other zeros and poles of f in B, of winding w_j. Near a simple zero z^*,\n\nf(z) = (z - z^*)q(z) qquad d = f(z^*) = q(z^*) = c prod_j (z^* - z_j)^w_j\n\nwith c free of zeros and poles in B, varying slowly. A track follows the germ (z^* d): when z^* and a simple z_j exchange places, z^* - z_j changes sign and arg d turns by pi. A step to p is accepted if\n\nz^*(p) - hat z(p) le tau qquad log d(p) - log hat d(p) le tfrac12\n\nwith hat z, hat d extrapolated from earlier samples (log hat d linear in p) and tau set by zres. As another locus approaches, log d turns faster and steps shorten, and tau shrinks against the distance to the nearest other locus,\n\nR = leftfrac2ffright_z^* = leftfracqqright_z^*\n\nBelow the floor delta, zfloor, nothing is told apart. Across a stretch with R  delta, the turn Delta = arg(d_textoutd_textin) tells what happened:\n\nDelta approx 0\nor pi: the loci passed or crossed. The track is followed through, and the branch lists the stretch in uncertified.\nDelta approx pmpi2\n: two loci of a kind met at a branch point, z_pm approx a pm bsqrtp - p_0, and which outgoing locus continues which incoming one has no answer. The track ends there, uncertified. Expected where real roots of a real f collide and leave as a complex pair.\n\nAt each knot, arriving tracks are matched to the seeds. A locus found at any knot is followed through the knots where the seeder missed it, and one that entered B between knots is followed back from where it was found.\n\nThese checks rest on estimates, not theorems: d and R are leading-order readings, and an exchange among three loci equally close could leave d unturned. The step control follows a priori path tracking for polynomial homotopies [TVV]; 1R is the leading term of Smale's gamma in certified tracking [BL].\n\n[TVV]: S. Telen, M. Van Barel, J. Verschelde, A robust numerical path tracking algorithm for polynomial homotopy continuation, SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 42 (2020), arXiv:1909.04984.\n\n[BL]: C. Beltrán, A. Leykin, Certified numerical homotopy tracking, Exp. Math. 21 (2012), 69–83, doi:10.1080/10586458.2011.606184.","title":"What is certified"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#The-count","page":"Continuation","text":"Tracks keep to their own loci beside an unseeded one, but do not report it. What notices a seeder that came up short is the argument principle at each knot,\n\nN(p_k) = frac12pi i oint_partial B fracff dz = textzeros - textpoles\n\ncounted with multiplicity, against sum_b w_b over the branches at p_k. The Survey's deficit lists the knots where they differ, and those where N cannot be read: a locus on partial B, or f unusable along it. N cannot see a zero and a pole missing together, nor anything between knots. An ArgumentPrinciple seeder computes N anyway; for others it costs one sampling of partial B per knot, skipped with certify = false.","title":"The count"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#Several-parameters","page":"Continuation","text":"The knots span a grid K_1 times dots times K_M. Every node is seeded, and every grid line, one p_i varying with the others at knots, is followed as above. A locus found at any node is followed along every line through the nodes it reaches.\n\nA branch is a set of node loci connected by tracks along any lines. Two zeros generically coincide only at isolated points of a parameter plane, and continuation around such a point swaps them: tracks around a grid cell holding one connect two loci of one node, and the branch has degree 2. A branch of degree 1 is a single-valued z^*(p).\n\nFor real f of real parameters, meetings lie along whole curves, where real roots collide and leave as a complex pair: the branches on either side end on the curve, uncertified at each grid line that crosses it.","title":"Several parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#What-it-can-miss","page":"Continuation","text":"A locus in B at no knot: one living only within (p_k p_k+1), or entering after p_K-1 and gone by p_K.\nA locus the seeder misses at every knot it is in B at. One found at any knot is followed to all the others it reaches.\nLoci of noisy f: the corrector needs f accurate to about 10^-10 relative, and to 10^-10m at a locus of multiplicity m. Tracks then end stuck: keep known multiple factors factored. At a branch cut tracks end stuck too.\nf\nthat jumps in p, in scale or phase (a piecewise definition, a normalisation that switches): d does not continue across the jump, and tracks end stuck there. A factor that varies rapidly in p only costs steps: divide it out.\nA knot placed exactly where loci meet cuts their branches there, uncertified. Move the knot.\nOver several parameters, what only the inside of a grid cell shows: loci in B only there, loci connected only there (separate branches), and two swap points in one cell, whose swaps undo each other (two branches of degree 1). A finer grid resolves each.","title":"What it can miss"},{"category":"section","location":"continuation/#Cost","page":"Continuation","text":"Seeding usually dominates, so the knots are the main cost. They serve only to discover loci: where none enters B between them, p_textlo p_texthi is enough. Close approaches between loci cost the most steps. Accuracy does not depend on zres, and cost only weakly.\n\nf(p) is called once per knot and once per step attempted, so setup work inside f(p) is paid per step and per locus.","title":"Cost"},{"category":"section","location":"#MeromorphicLoci.jl","page":"Home","text":"Find the zeros and poles of a meromorphic function f(z) in a box of the complex plane,  at fixed parameters or as loci z*(p) that move with parameters p.","title":"MeromorphicLoci.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quickstart","page":"Home","text":"using MeromorphicLoci\n\nf(z) = (z^2 - 1) * (z - 0.5im) / (z - 2)\nr = findzeropoles(f, (-3 - 1im, 3 + 1im), AAA())   # the box by opposite corners\n# windings: > 0 for a zero, < 0 for a pole, |winding| = multiplicity\n@assert r.points ≈ [-1, 0.5im, 1, 2] && r.windings == [1, 1, 1, -1]\n\nOver a parameter, findloci takes g(p...) returning the function of z:\n\n# a zero and a pole that circle the origin as p varies\n# plus a fixed zero–pole pair\ng(p) = z -> (z - 0.80 * cispi(p)) * (z + 0.10 + 0.12im) /\n            ((z - 0.804 * cispi(-p + 0.3)) * (z + 0.13 + 0.12im))\nzbox = (-0.98 - 0.98im, 0.98 + 0.98im)\npbox = ((0.0, 1.0),)                    # one (lo, hi) or vector of knots per parameter\ns = findloci(g, zbox, pbox, AAA())\n@assert sort([b.winding for b in s]) == [-1, -1, 1, 1]   # the moving pair and the fixed pair\n# nothing missed or ambiguous\n@assert isempty(s.deficit) && all(b -> isempty(b.uncertified), s)\n\ns holds one Branch per locus: a vector of samples x, sorted by  the parameter tuple x.p, with x.z on the locus.\n\n(Image: zero and pole loci over the parameter)","title":"Quickstart"},{"category":"section","location":"#Interface","page":"Home","text":"findzeros(f, zdomain, alg; polish, mapper)       # zeros of f(z)\nfindpoles(f, zdomain, alg; polish, mapper)       # poles\nfindzeropoles(f, zdomain, alg; polish, mapper)   # both, from one search\nfindloci(f, zdomain, pdomain, alg; mapper)       # loci z*(p) of zeros and poles of f(p...)(z)\n\nThe point finders return (; points, windings, converged, unresolved, nevals, …); findloci returns a Survey of Branches (?MeromorphicLoci.Survey, ?MeromorphicLoci.Branch).","title":"Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"#Algorithms","page":"Home","text":"AAA(; nsamples, tol, …) fits a rational function to samples of f. Use it first: it costs about nsamples evaluations plus a few dozen per point found, whatever the box size, where ArgumentPrinciple costs at least 2·area/minsep²; and it resolves zero–pole pairs a few times closer than its sample spacing.\nArgumentPrinciple(; zres, minsep, …) refines cells near the zeros and poles by the argument principle. Use it to cross-check AAA (Checks and cost).\nContinuation(seed; …) runs the point finder seed at a few parameter values, the knots, and follows each locus between them with Muller's method. findloci with a point finder alg is short for Continuation(alg).","title":"Algorithms"},{"category":"section","location":"#AAA","page":"Home","text":"The fit, its cost and what it can miss: aaa.md. benchmark/aaa.jl compares it with RationalFunctionApproximation.jl and BaryRational.jl.","title":"AAA"},{"category":"section","location":"#Continuation","page":"Home","text":"The quickstart's moving pole passes 0.004 from the zero, yet their branches stay apart: following tells loci apart down to zfloor (?Continuation), far below the sample spacing; closer approaches are listed in uncertified. A nonempty s.deficit means the count disagreed with the branches at some knot, or could not be read there.\n\nOver several parameters, the knots form a grid: loci are found at its nodes and followed along its lines.\n\n# the pole surface passes 0.004–0.05 from the zero surface\nh(p, q) = z -> (z - 0.8 * cispi(p)) / (z - (0.804 + 0.05q) * cispi(-p + 0.3))\ns2 = findloci(h, zbox, ((0.0, 1.0), (0.0, 1.0)), AAA())\n@assert sort([b.winding for b in s2]) == [-1, 1] && all(b -> b.degree == 1, s2)\n\nFeatures confined to the inside of a grid cell go unseen; a finer grid resolves them. How it works and what is certified: continuation.md.","title":"Continuation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Argument-principle","page":"Home","text":"r = findzeropoles(z -> (z - 0.3) * (z + 0.4im)^2 / (z - 0.6), (-1 - 1im, 1 + 1im),\n                  ArgumentPrinciple(zres = 0.01))\n@assert r.windings == [2, 1, -1] && isempty(r.unresolved)   # −0.4i, 0.3, 0.6\n@assert r.deficit == 0\n\nCells are refined down to zres wherever the phase of f winds around them, then grouped and labelled by the winding of f around each group. A zero–pole pair closer than minsep can be missed entirely: one coarse cell holds both, and their windings cancel. A minsep below their separation finds it, at higher cost:\n\npair(z) = (z + 0.10 + 0.12im) / (z + 0.13 + 0.12im)   # 0.03 apart\n@assert isempty(findzeropoles(pair, zbox, ArgumentPrinciple(zres = 0.02)).points)\n@assert findzeropoles(pair, zbox, ArgumentPrinciple(zres = 0.02, minsep = 0.025)).windings == [-1, 1]\n\nHow it works: argument_principle.md. RootsAndPoles.jl solves the same problem with SA-GRPF on a Delaunay triangulation; benchmark/compare.jl tracks the loci of a dispersion relation over a wavenumber range with one findloci call and with 128 independent RootsAndPoles slices.","title":"Argument principle"},{"category":"section","location":"#Checks-and-cost","page":"Home","text":"Completeness. Every count is of zeros minus poles, so a zero and a pole missed together escape it. Factor out what you know, e.g. search z -> f(z) * (z - a) for the zeros beside a known pole a: fewer points to find, and fewer pairs to cancel.\nArgumentPrinciple: r.deficit == 0 && isempty(r.unresolved) leaves only pairs closer than minsep unfound.\nAAA: no count; r.fitconverged says only that the fit met tol (?AAA). Cross-check with ArgumentPrinciple where a miss matters.\nContinuation: counts at every knot with either point finder: isempty(s.deficit). Otherwise raise nsamples for AAA, or lower minsep for ArgumentPrinciple.\nExpensive thread-safe f. Pass mapper an order-preserving threaded map, such as tmap(f, xs) = fetch.([Threads.@spawn f(x) for x in xs]) or OhMyThreads.jl's tmap. findloci also calls f(p...) through it, which must then be thread-safe too. In Continuation, finding at the knots dominates the cost, and only knots discover loci:  if no locus enters the box, pass the knots [lo, hi].","title":"Checks and cost"},{"category":"section","location":"#Branch-cuts","page":"Home","text":"Neither point finder detects a branch cut as such. ArgumentPrinciple reports it as one unresolved point if its phase jump skips a quadrant (sqrt), and otherwise misses it silently (cbrt), along with any zero at its branch point. AAA fills it with a string of interleaved zeros and poles.","title":"Branch cuts"}]
}
